                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "id": "tiL0n",
                          "x": 178,
                          "y": 93,
                          "name": "Chapter",
                          "fill": "#8A8A8A",
                          "content": "CHAPTER FOUR",
                          "fontFamily": "$font",
                          "fontSize": 11.16,
                          "fontWeight": "600",
                          "letterSpacing": 1.8599999999999999
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "id": "d6xEK",
                          "x": 178,
                          "y": 115.32,
                          "name": "Heading",
                          "fill": "#161616",
                          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                          "width": 620,
                          "content": "How systems hold together",
                          "lineHeight": 1.1,
                          "fontFamily": "Newsreader",
                          "fontSize": 45.88,
                          "fontWeight": "normal"
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "id": "VXX2Y",
                          "x": 178,
                          "y": 183.52,
                          "name": "Body",
                          "fill": "#242424",
                          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                          "width": 620,
                          "content": "A system is more than the sum of its parts. It is a set of elements, interconnected in a way that produces its own pattern of behavior over time. Stocks are the elements you can see and count at any given time. Flows are the actions that change them, filling and draining them as the days pass.\n\nA bathtub is the simplest example. The water in the tub is a stock. The faucet is an inflow and the drain is an outflow. If the two are equal the level holds still, though the water itself keeps moving.\n\nFeedback loops give a system its character. A balancing loop pushes a stock toward a goal, the way a thermostat holds a room steady through a winter night.",
                          "lineHeight": 1.46,
                          "fontFamily": "Newsreader",
                          "fontSize": 21.7,
                          "fontWeight": "normal"
                        },
